  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 PM EDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nEarly this afternoon upper air analysis and GOES-R water vapor \nimagery showed relatively zonal flow over the north-central U.S. \nwith a shortwave trough closing off over MN/WI. While the strongest \nlarge scale ascent remained south of the U.P., closer to the low \npressure. Clouds were spreading northeast across the area along deep \nmoisture, with PWATs running around 1.8\" (close to the 97th \npercentile of climo). Rain showers had been ongoing much of the day \nover the western U.P. along and south of a deformation axis, \nespecially closer to the WI/MI border. Over the central and east the \nclouds were progressively thinner, allowing for surface heating and \ndestabilization, with temps ranging from the low 70s west to the low \n80s east. SPC RAP analysis showed 1000-2000 J/kg surface based cape \nover the central and eastern U.P., along and ahead of a lake breeze \npushing south off of Lake Superior. A few thunderstorms were already \nforming in this area.\n\nLow pressure will continue to slowly move from northern IA/southern \nMN to near Lake Erie through the weekend. Deep moisture and \ninstability (~500-1500 J/kg) will remain over the area through this \nevening (PWATs of around 1.8\" and surface dewpoints up to 70F). This \nwill combine with an advancing Lake Superior lake breeze this \nafternoon to trigger thunderstorm development over the central and \neastern U.P., especially over the interior along and ahead of the \nboundary. Elevated instability along with a notable deformation zone \nwill also trigger showers, and possibly a rumble of thunder, on the \ncool side of the lake breeze boundary (Lake Superior lakeshore \nareas). 0-6km shear is practically non-existant, less than 10kt as \nthe flow through the layer is very weak. This will result in slow \nshower/storm motion through the evening. Any showers and \nthunderstorms that form will be capable of very heavy rainfall \nrates, with HREF guidance PMM showing areas of over 2\" QPF and \nisolated amounts to 4\". Given the deep warm and moist layer and slow \nstorm motions isolated heavy rainfall of 2+\" seems reasonable. \nHowever, trying to pin down to any single location is difficult. The \nbest chance for the more extreme rainfall rates will be ahead of the \nlake breeze boundary. Given the expected isolated nature of the \nheaviest rainfall feel a flood watch is not warranted but will \ncontinue messaging through other channels.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms should shift to the eastern U.P. by late \ntonight and diminish in (rain rate) intensity. Patchy fog may \ndevelop overnight as the low levels cool along with weak flow and \nample moisture. Expect a winds shift to the north slightly after \ndaybreak. This will help clear out the fog except it may continue in \nthe north wind upslope areas (highlands of Marquette County and the \nKeweenaw) and along Lake Superior should fog form over the lake \ntonight. Temperatures will be cooler, highs in the upper 60s north \nto 70s south. Locations along Lake Superior could see temperatures \nfall into the low to mid 60s in the late afternoon as cooler air \ncontinues to push south behind a secondary cold front. Showers will \nbe possible as well in the east, closer to the low pressure. \nAdditional light showers or perhaps a period of drizzle can be \nexpected over the north-central, especially Marquette County.\n\nNotably drier air can be expected for Sunday into Monday with \ndewpoints in the 50s, resulting in more comfortable conditions. \nTemperatures will be in the 70s on Sunday and upper 70s to low 80s \non Monday (close to seasonal averages).\n\nOn Tuesday humidity will begin increasing ahead of an approaching \ncold front. Showers and thunderstorms may accompany the front on \nTuesday but the risk for organized convection remains uncertain at \nthis point in time, with global models showing lower instability and \nshear. It will fall back into the 70s for Wednesday behind the front \nwith a rebound again on Thursday along with a slight chance for \nthunderstorms.",
